<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Release-only removals.

      Neither permission is declared by this app. Both arrive from prebuilt
      AARs in the dependency graph, and both are dead weight in a build that
      never opens a socket: reminders are scheduled by the OS, storage is local
      SQLite, and there is no server anywhere in the feature (FR-034, FR-058).

      Left in place they would show "Full network access" on the Play listing —
      directly contradicting the Data safety answers this app gives, which is
      the kind of mismatch that costs trust for no benefit.

      The debug source set adds INTERNET back, because Metro serves the JS
      bundle over the network during development. That split is the whole point
      of putting these here rather than in the main manifest.

      NOTE: verify with a real release build before shipping. If a native
      module turns out to touch the network at startup it will throw a
      SecurityException, and the fix is to delete the matching line below.
    -->
    <uses-permission
      android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"
      tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission
      android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
      tools:node="remove" />

</manifest>
